\section{Two different meanings of systems programming}

Richard A.\ Kelsey's \emph{Pre-Scheme: A Scheme Dialect for Systems Programming} uses Scheme for code that operates on machine data. Its compiler evaluates top-level forms at compile time, reconstructs types with a polymorphic type system, and specializes sufficiently restricted higher-order code for translation to C. A programmer can use full Scheme while computing the residual program. The generated program, however, has no automatic garbage collection of its own. Local tail recursion is guaranteed for the documented \texttt{let} and \texttt{letrec} lambda bindings, and other calls can be explicitly marked for proper tail transfer in tail position; an arbitrary call through C is not automatically a tail call.

Olin Shivers's \emph{A Scheme Shell} uses Scheme for a different layer: arranging Unix processes and their file descriptors. The scsh process notation uses S-expressions, but those expressions form a language of pipelines and redirections embedded in Scheme, not ordinary procedure applications. The process specification determines how to wire and invoke other programs; Scheme supplies the data and control that build the specification. Pre-Scheme removes abstraction from an executable to obtain machine operations. The shell adds abstraction above the operating system to make orchestration tractable.

\section{Worked specialization: an arithmetic instruction}

Kelsey's example from Scheme 48 uses a higher-order helper to implement arithmetic instructions. Fix an operation such as addition during compilation, and produce code which checks two tagged arguments, extracts the fixnums, invokes the known operation, and either tags a representable result or transfers to a failure handler with the original arguments. The function and the success and failure continuations are a useful abstraction in the source. Where the arguments to that abstraction are known, beta reduction and branch lowering can remove generic call machinery. This can leave an ordinary dispatch case in C, even though the instruction was specified with higher-order code.

The invariant is that a fast-path result must decode to the sum of the two inputs, and a slow-path transfer must receive the untouched operands in their original order. Test small positive and negative operands, the greatest representable fixnum plus one, and a value with a non-fixnum tag. A C translation must arrange not to overflow a signed machine type while implementing the overflow check; widening or checking bounds before adding are different ways to discharge that obligation. The Scheme 48 collector is itself runtime code written in Pre-Scheme. It manages the \emph{guest} heap and does not imply a collector for storage used by the compiled systems program. Match tests against a hosted Scheme run, but separately check buffer lifetimes and representation bounds that a hosted run may conceal.

\section{Worked script: a descriptor graph}

In scsh notation a pipeline can sort the lines of a selected file and count occurrences without concatenating a command string:

\begin{verbatim}
(run (| (sort ,input-path) (uniq -c))
     (> ,output-path))
\end{verbatim}

A process form of the shape \verb|(prog arg ...)| is implicitly backquoted, so the commas evaluate Scheme variables and each pathname becomes one argument to its destination. The pipeline connects the first process's standard output to the second process's standard input; the redirection sends the final output to a file. A pathname containing spaces does not become several arguments. This is independent of how an individual command interprets its remaining operands: a name starting with a hyphen may still be treated as an option by that command. Test with a fixture of repeated lines and set a fixed locale if the check depends on sort order.

At the Unix level, attach the pipe endpoints to the child descriptors and close all unneeded copies. A reader sees EOF only after all writers close; a leaked write descriptor in the parent can hang an otherwise correct pipeline. Test an empty file as well as one containing many lines. Also test a failure in the first stage separately from failure of the last stage; a single exit status must not be assumed to summarize both. The language abstraction is doing its job when the source says how the processes connect and the lowering takes responsibility for the descriptor bookkeeping.

\section{A runtime at the hardware boundary}

Samuel Yvon and Marc Feeley's \emph{Running Scheme On Bare Metal}, from the 2020 Scheme and Functional Programming Workshop, describes Mimosa, which uses Gambit and keeps runtime services such as garbage collection and threads. This is a third choice, not a restriction to Pre-Scheme or scripting over a host OS. An interrupt can arrive during a partially completed allocation, when the Scheme handler must not run. Mimosa registers the event from low-level code, and the running Scheme thread can invoke the handler at a compiler-inserted polling point. The boundary trades some delivery latency for a consistent runtime state. Test for delayed delivery both during allocation and in a loop with no allocation; safe deferral is not enough if a busy path never polls.

