\section{Budget Poll Distance Without Splitting an Allocation}

Consider an illustrative code generator, not the Mimosa polling algorithm. It receives a control-flow graph partitioned into chunks separated by boundaries where the runtime invariant holds and all roots can be described. Each block entry is such a boundary. Each chunk has a conservative bound on its executed instruction count, including any branch at its end; it may include a short allocation sequence that must run uninterrupted. To make the policy simple, poll at every block entry, then add polls inside blocks that contain several chunks. The output either has at most \(B\) ordinary instructions between polls or reports that the requested budget cannot be met with the given boundaries.

\begin{verbatim}
place_polls(graph, B):
  for block in graph:
    # Every incoming path starts a new interval.
    insert poll at entry of block
    spent = 0
    for chunk in block:
      if chunk.bound > B: return NeedsSmallerChunk
      if spent + chunk.bound > B:
        # A boundary has a valid root map.
        insert poll before chunk
        spent = 0
      spent = spent + chunk.bound
  return graph with polls
\end{verbatim}

For a self-loop made of chunks of costs \(3,2,3,1\) and \(B=5\), the emitted polls occur before chunk one and before chunk three. Assuming each chunk uses its full bound, a trace records polls after instruction counts \(0,5,9,14,18,\ldots\), not counting the polls themselves. An event posted just after the poll at \(5\) can first be observed at \(9\); no handler runs halfway through the three-instruction chunk. An indivisible chunk of cost \(6\) produces \texttt{NeedsSmallerChunk}, rather than a poll at an unsafe point. In a real compiler, a failed planning pass would discard its tentative insertions.

\Cref{fig:visual-168} marks both legal chunk boundaries and the
subset at which the budget algorithm inserts polls.

