The running sum bounds the path segment since the most recent poll, and every back edge reaches an entry poll even when the loop never allocates. The construction takes one pass over the chunks but can insert unnecessary polls on short blocks. This is not a wall-clock guarantee: a blocking foreign call, an unbounded instruction latency, or time spent in handlers needs separate treatment. In particular, assigning a unit cost to a call is sound only if its execution is accounted for separately.
